/**
  @page fem-ciarlet Ciarlet's definition of a finite element
  @brief The mathematical foundation of finite elements

  @m_keywords{Ciarlet, Finite Element, Basis Functions, Reference Element}

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section fem-ciarlet-intro Introduction

  The finite element method (FEM) is a powerful technique for numerically
  solving partial differential equations. At its heart lies the concept of a
  **finite element**, which provides a systematic way to construct
  piecewise-polynomial approximations on a mesh.

  The most widely used formal definition of a finite element was introduced by
  Philippe G. Ciarlet. This definition provides a clean mathematical
  framework that separates three concerns:

  1. **Geometry**: on which shape is the element defined?
  2. **Approximation**: which polynomial space is used?
  3. **Degrees of freedom**: how are functions in the space uniquely determined?

  This separation is precisely mirrored in Rodin's architecture: the
  @ref Rodin::Geometry "Geometry" module handles shapes, the
  @ref Rodin::Variational "Variational" module defines polynomial spaces and
  DOFs, and the @ref Rodin::Assembly "Assembly" module puts them together.

  @section fem-ciarlet-definition The Ciarlet Triple

  A **finite element** in the sense of Ciarlet is defined as a triple
  @f$ (K, P, \Sigma) @f$ where:

  - @f$ K @f$ is a **geometric domain** — a compact, connected subset of
    @f$ \mathbb{R}^d @f$ with nonempty interior (e.g. a triangle,
    tetrahedron, quadrilateral). This is also called the **reference element**.
    In Rodin, reference element geometry is defined by the
    @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope::Type "Polytope::Type" enumeration
    (Triangle, Tetrahedron, Quadrilateral, etc.).

  - @f$ P @f$ is a finite-dimensional **function space** defined on @f$ K @f$
    — typically a space of polynomials of a certain degree. For instance,
    @f$ P = \mathbb{P}_k(K) @f$, the space of polynomials of degree at most
    @f$ k @f$, whose dimension is:
    @f[
      \dim \mathbb{P}_k(\mathbb{R}^d) = \binom{k + d}{d}
    @f]
    For @f$ d = 2 @f$: @f$ \dim \mathbb{P}_1 = 3 @f$,
    @f$ \dim \mathbb{P}_2 = 6 @f$.
    For @f$ d = 3 @f$: @f$ \dim \mathbb{P}_1 = 4 @f$,
    @f$ \dim \mathbb{P}_2 = 10 @f$.

  - @f$ \Sigma = \{ \sigma_1, \sigma_2, \ldots, \sigma_n \} @f$ is a set of
    **degrees of freedom** (DOFs) — linear functionals
    @f$ \sigma_i : P \to \mathbb{R} @f$ — such that any function
    @f$ p \in P @f$ is uniquely determined by the values
    @f$ \sigma_1(p), \ldots, \sigma_n(p) @f$.

  The key requirement is that the DOFs must form a **basis** for the dual
  space @f$ P' @f$. This means @f$ \dim(P) = n @f$, and the map
  @f[
    p \mapsto (\sigma_1(p), \ldots, \sigma_n(p))
  @f]
  is an isomorphism from @f$ P @f$ to @f$ \mathbb{R}^n @f$. Equivalently,
  if @f$ \sigma_i(p) = 0 @f$ for all @f$ i @f$, then @f$ p = 0 @f$.
  This property is called **unisolvence**.

  @section fem-ciarlet-basis Basis Functions

  Given a Ciarlet triple @f$ (K, P, \Sigma) @f$, there exists a unique set of
  **basis functions** @f$ \{ \phi_1, \ldots, \phi_n \} \subset P @f$ such that:

  @f[
    \sigma_i(\phi_j) = \delta_{ij}
  @f]

  where @f$ \delta_{ij} @f$ is the Kronecker delta. This is called the
  **nodal basis** of the finite element. These basis functions are the shape
  functions that are used to represent any function in the finite element
  space.

  Any function @f$ p \in P @f$ can then be written as:

  @f[
    p = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sigma_i(p) \, \phi_i
  @f]

  The values @f$ \sigma_i(p) @f$ are the **degrees of freedom** (DOF values)
  of @f$ p @f$. In Rodin, these DOF values are stored as the coefficient
  vector of a @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction".

  @section fem-ciarlet-examples Common Finite Elements

  @subsection fem-ciarlet-p1 P1 (Piecewise Linear) Elements

  The simplest and most widely used finite element is the **P1 element**
  on a triangle:

  - @f$ K @f$ = a triangle with vertices @f$ a_1, a_2, a_3 @f$
  - @f$ P = \mathbb{P}_1(K) @f$ = polynomials of degree @f$ \leq 1 @f$
    (i.e. @f$ p(x, y) = \alpha + \beta x + \gamma y @f$), dimension 3
  - @f$ \Sigma = \{ \sigma_1, \sigma_2, \sigma_3 \} @f$ where
    @f$ \sigma_i(p) = p(a_i) @f$ (point evaluations at vertices)

  The basis functions are the **barycentric coordinates**
  @f$ \lambda_1, \lambda_2, \lambda_3 @f$, satisfying
  @f$ \lambda_i(a_j) = \delta_{ij} @f$. Each basis function is 1 at one
  vertex and 0 at the other two, varying linearly between them.

  In Rodin, a P1 finite element space is created with:

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);
  // Number of DOFs = number of vertices
  std::cout << Vh.getSize() << std::endl;
  @endcode

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1" class constructs one DOF per mesh
  vertex, producing a space of continuous piecewise linear functions that
  is @f$ H^1 @f$-conforming.

  @subsection fem-ciarlet-p0 P0 (Piecewise Constant) Elements

  An even simpler element is the **P0 element**, used for discontinuous
  quantities:

  - @f$ K @f$ = a polytope (triangle, tetrahedron, etc.)
  - @f$ P = \mathbb{P}_0(K) @f$ = constant functions, dimension 1
  - @f$ \Sigma = \{ \sigma_1 \} @f$ where @f$ \sigma_1(p) @f$ is the value
    of the constant, often defined as the average over @f$ K @f$

  In Rodin:

  @code{.cpp}
  P0 Wh(mesh);
  // Number of DOFs = number of cells
  std::cout << Wh.getSize() << std::endl;
  @endcode

  The @ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0" class constructs one DOF per mesh
  cell. Functions in P0 are discontinuous across cell boundaries, making this
  space a subspace of @f$ L^2(\Omega) @f$ (not @f$ H^1 @f$).

  @subsection fem-ciarlet-hk Higher-Order Elements (H1<k>)

  For polynomial degree @f$ k \geq 2 @f$, additional DOFs are placed on edges
  and (for @f$ k \geq 3 @f$) on cell interiors. For example, the P2 element
  on a triangle has:

  - @f$ K @f$ = a triangle
  - @f$ P = \mathbb{P}_2(K) @f$ = quadratic polynomials, dimension 6
  - @f$ \Sigma @f$ = point evaluations at the 3 vertices and 3 edge midpoints

  In Rodin, higher-order elements are provided by the
  @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1<k>" class:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Quadratic (P2) finite element space
  H1 Vh(std::integral_constant<size_t, 2>{}, mesh);
  @endcode

  The polynomial order @f$ k @f$ is a compile-time template parameter.

  @section fem-ciarlet-global From Local to Global

  The Ciarlet definition describes a **local** finite element on a single
  reference cell @f$ \hat{K} @f$. To construct a **global** finite element
  space on an entire mesh @f$ \mathcal{T}_h @f$, we:

  1. **Define** a reference element @f$ (\hat{K}, \hat{P}, \hat{\Sigma}) @f$
  2. **Map** it to each physical cell @f$ K \in \mathcal{T}_h @f$ using a
     geometric transformation @f$ F_K : \hat{K} \to K @f$. In Rodin, the
     Jacobian of this transformation is accessible through
     @ref Rodin::Geometry::Point "Point::getJacobian()".
  3. **Assemble** local DOFs into global DOFs, enforcing inter-element
     continuity conditions at shared entities (vertices, edges, faces)

  The result is a finite-dimensional subspace @f$ V_h @f$ of the function
  space in which we seek our approximate solution.

  The assembly of local DOFs into global DOFs is handled automatically when
  you create a finite element space. For example, `P1 Vh(mesh)` constructs
  the global DOF numbering by identifying vertex-based DOFs on shared mesh
  vertices.

  @section fem-ciarlet-conformity Conformity

  Different types of inter-element continuity give rise to different types
  of conforming finite element spaces:

  | Conformity | Continuity requirement | Typical use | Rodin class |
  |------------|----------------------|-------------|-------------|
  | @f$ H^1 @f$-conforming | Continuous across element boundaries | Scalar PDEs (Poisson, heat) | @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1", @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1" |
  | @f$ H(\mathrm{div}) @f$-conforming | Normal component continuous | Mixed methods, fluid flow | — |
  | @f$ H(\mathrm{curl}) @f$-conforming | Tangential component continuous | Electromagnetics | — |
  | @f$ L^2 @f$ (no conformity) | No continuity required | Discontinuous Galerkin, indicators | @ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0" |

  Rodin currently provides @f$ H^1 @f$-conforming elements (P1, H1) and
  @f$ L^2 @f$ elements (P0). The P1 and H1 spaces produce global functions
  that are continuous across element boundaries: values at shared vertices
  (and shared edge/face DOFs for higher order) are identified.

  @section fem-ciarlet-rodin Connection to Rodin

  In Rodin, the Ciarlet definition is realized through the interaction of
  several classes:

  | Ciarlet component | Mathematical object | Rodin class |
  |-------------------|---------------------|-------------|
  | @f$ K @f$ | Reference element geometry | @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope "Polytope" + @ref Rodin::Geometry::Polytope::Type "Polytope::Type" |
  | @f$ P @f$ | Local polynomial space | Defined internally by @ref Rodin::Variational::P0 "P0", @ref Rodin::Variational::P1 "P1", @ref Rodin::Variational::H1 "H1" |
  | @f$ \Sigma @f$ | Degrees of freedom | DOF numbering managed by the finite element space class |
  | @f$ V_h @f$ | Global finite element space | The space object itself (e.g. `P1 Vh(mesh)`) |
  | @f$ u_h \in V_h @f$ | Discrete function | @ref Rodin::Variational::GridFunction "GridFunction" |
  | @f$ u @f$ (unknown) | Trial function | @ref Rodin::Variational::TrialFunction "TrialFunction" |
  | @f$ v @f$ (weight) | Test function | @ref Rodin::Variational::TestFunction "TestFunction" |

  @code{.cpp}
  // The mesh provides the cells K and the geometric transformations F_K
  Mesh mesh;
  mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Triangle, {16, 16});

  // P1 defines the local element (K, P1, point evaluations at vertices)
  // and assembles them into a global space Vh
  P1 Vh(mesh);

  // Trial and test functions live in this global space
  TrialFunction u(Vh);
  TestFunction  v(Vh);
  @endcode

